          "documents": [
            {
              "type": "Consult Note",
              "title": "Gastroenterology clinic note with paracentesis",
              "author": "gastroenterology",
              "date": "2023-02-10T09:20",
              "body": "GI CLINIC — 2/6/2023, note faxed 2/10/2023.\n\nSeen a year after his hemorrhoid workup. Two months of gradually worsening abdominal swelling and discomfort. Eating less. Denies nausea, vomiting or a change in bowel habits. No travel, no ill contacts. Adherent to furosemide and spironolactone. Ongoing alcohol use.\n\nMeds: furosemide 40 daily, spironolactone 100 daily, propranolol 20 BID, testosterone cypionate 200 mg IM q2wk, vitamin K 10 mg daily, lactulose PRN. NKDA.\n\nExam: cachectic. Palmar erythema. Abdomen distended with fluid wave and shifting dullness. No peripheral edema today.\n\nLabs: albumin 2.8, sodium 135, creatinine 1.1, total bilirubin 3.2, INR 1.5.\n\nProcedure: diagnostic paracentesis, left lower quadrant, ultrasound-marked, after consent. Clear straw-colored fluid. Ascitic albumin 1.2 g/dL; serum-ascites albumin gradient 1.6, consistent with portal hypertension. Cell count without evidence of infection. No complications.\n\nImpression: alcohol-related cirrhosis with portal hypertensive ascites; recent PCP ultrasound without liver lesion.\nPlan: continue current diuretic doses — his blood pressure limits increases. Two-gram sodium diet reviewed. Abstinence strongly advised. Return in one year or sooner if needed."
            }
