          "documents": [
            {
              "type": "Consult Note",
              "title": "Infectious Diseases annual follow-up",
              "author": "infectious diseases",
              "date": "2024-02-05T11:15",
              "body": "ID CLINIC — annual visit 1/29/2024, letter 2/5/2024.\n\nFollowing for hepatitis C, treated with sofosbuvir/velpatasvir in 2021–22 with sustained virologic response, on a background of alcohol-related cirrhosis with portal hypertension, Kallmann syndrome and internal hemorrhoids (banded 2022). He has no acute concerns; some fatigue now and then. He continues to drink daily.\n\nMeds: furosemide, spironolactone, vitamin K, propranolol 20 mg BID, testosterone cypionate 200 mg IM every 2 weeks, lactulose 30 mL BID PRN. NKDA.\n\nVitals stable. Exam: mild ascites. Spider angiomas on the upper trunk; palmar erythema.\n\nLabs: platelets 110,000, INR 1.2, albumin 3.2, total bilirubin 2.1, AST 80, ALT 75. HCV RNA not detected. Hepatitis B surface antigen negative, surface antibody negative.\n\nImmunizations: tetanus booster 8 years ago (2016). No record of hepatitis A, hepatitis B or pneumococcal vaccination.\n\nAssessment: HCV remains cured. Cirrhosis with no immunity to hepatitis B and no record of hepatitis A or pneumococcal vaccine.\nRecommendations to primary care: hepatitis A series, hepatitis B series and a pneumococcal conjugate vaccine (PCV20). Tdap due in 2026. We will see him again only if needed; repeat HCV RNA if there is any new exposure."
            }
